//! member: true
//!
//! std.json — the JSON data model, its accessors, and the serializer.
//! Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **The module is a READER now, and that is this sprint's whole story.**
//! For eight sprints this was a complete DOM that could not be built from
//! text: `parse` and `unescape` were reviewed contracts, and `escape`
//! carried a refusal. §14's debt clause — a contract ships in the sprint
//! AFTER its blocker closes, one sprint of grace and no more — came due
//! here on all three at once, and all three are below as code.
//!
//! - **`parse`** was blocked by F-0037 (an enum returned through an error
//!   row took the miss path on every call), closed at lupin 0.1.10 and
//!   spent at sc13 on `get`/`at`. Its scanner has been writable since
//!   sc09; the SIGNATURE was what was missing.
//! - **`unescape`** was blocked by F-0057 — the walk was always fine, the
//!   walk's OUTPUT was not, because decoding `é` means building `"é"` out
//!   of the number 233 and nothing in the language built a `str` from a
//!   number. s81 landed the prelude's validating `str_from_utf8`, and
//!   **lupin 0.1.12 resolves it too** (F-0075 closed), which matters more
//!   here than anywhere: the interpreter is this module's only executing
//!   lane, so a primitive the compilers alone had would have left
//!   `unescape` with no lane at all (sc10's `float_at` rule).
//! - **`escape` is TOTAL** and its `boundary` tag is gone. The walk is a
//!   byte walk now: the four characters JSON must escape are ASCII, every
//!   byte of a multi-byte code point is ≥ 0x80 and therefore never one of
//!   them, and a run between two escapes is a slice at offsets this module
//!   computed. So the function needs no code-point measurement at all —
//!   which is why it was reachable a full wave before anyone thought.
//!
//! **The signature change that follows, said out loud.** `escape`'s row
//! disappears, so `stringify` and `stringify_pretty` go from
//! `{boundary, deep}` to `{deep}` and `quote` becomes total. Every caller
//! that wrote `else "?"` still compiles — a narrower row is a subtype at a
//! `?` boundary and an `else` over one tag reads the same — but a caller
//! matching on `boundary` by name does not, and there is no deprecation
//! period in a library at this stage. It is one tag, removed once, in the
//! sprint that made it removable.
//!
//! **What `parse` costs the reader is one number-tier tag**, and the
//! sc05 contract did not have it: the row is `{syntax, deep, overflow}`
//! rather than `{syntax, deep}`. `overflow` is what a magnitude with no
//! `f64` raises (`1e400`), because `std.fmt.decimal.parse_float` refuses
//! it rather than answering infinity, and this module refuses to turn
//! that into "the text is not JSON" — the two are different sentences and
//! §12 gives a distinct actionable failure its own tag. RFC 8259 §6
//! permits an implementation to limit the range it accepts; it does not
//! permit a parser to lie about which document it was handed.
//!
//! **The value model.** `Value` is one recursive enum with seven
//! variants: `Null`, `Bool(bool)`, `Int(int)`, `Num(f64)`, `Str(str)`,
//! `Arr(List[Value])` and `Obj(Map[str, Value])`. The number posture is
//! the sprint contract's: integers keep their exactness in `Int` and only
//! non-integral (or out-of-range) numbers become `Num`, so a document
//! carrying a 64-bit id round-trips through wolf without passing through
//! a `f64` that cannot hold it. The i53 boundary is where the two
//! postures visibly differ and `tests/json/number_posture_and_depth.lu` pins
//! it, from the writing side, with `tests/json/parse_shapes.lu` pinning the
//! reading side.
//!
//! **Constructors are functions, not `Value.Int(3)`.** An enum's variants
//! cannot be named from outside the declaring module at these pins —
//! `json.Value.Int(3)` is "`Value` is an enum; traits, enums and
//! type-level items have no dynamic semantics here" (F-0029) — so the
//! seven `from_*`/`null` constructors below exist to give importers any
//! way at all to build a value. They are not the API the contract
//! imagined; they are the API the language currently allows, and the
//! filing says so.
//!
//! **Ordering.** `Obj` is a `Map`, whose iteration order is unspecified
//! (§8), so `stringify`'s key order is unspecified too and no test in this
//! repo asserts it for a multi-key object. That is a promise deliberately
//! not made rather than an omission; when the ordering question settles
//! upstream, this module states whatever the answer is.
//!
//! **The wolfc lane is dark for this module, by construction**: an
//! imported module that PRODUCES an enum value is `unsupported` in the
//! checked tier and has no recorded type natively (F-0029, F-0012), and
//! `Map` is not typed by the compiler at all. Every test here is
//! `lupin = run, wolfc = unsupported, native = unsupported`, exactly as
//! the sprint contract's non-target predicted.
//!
//! **The scanner's tag is `syntax`, not `parse`, and the reason is a
//! measurement rather than a preference.** A row tag is resolved as an
//! ordinary name at the raise site, so a tag that shares a name with
//! anything in scope silently becomes that thing (F-0036). This module now
//! declares `pub fn parse`, so a `parse` TAG raised anywhere in it would
//! resolve to that function and ride out as a value with no `else` firing
//! and no diagnostic. Re-measured at this sprint's pins rather than
//! inherited: the compiler closed its half at sc07 and **lupin still hands
//! back the colliding thing**, silently. So `unescape` raises `syntax`
//! too, which reads better anyway — a bad `\q` escape IS a syntax error in
//! the document it came from.
//!
//! **What is deliberately NOT accepted.** No comments, no trailing commas,
//! no leading `+`, no bare `.5`, no `NaN`/`Infinity` spellings, no raw
//! control character inside a string: RFC 8259's grammar, strictly. Every
//! one of them is a `syntax` miss, because a library that guesses at
//! extensions reads a different language from the one its callers' other
//! tools read. There is also no offset in the tag — `syntax` is a
//! payload-free mark until §12's payload conventions reach this module,
//! and when the position arrives the tag GAINS a payload rather than a
//! sibling (§12's rule 4).
//!
//! **Duplicate keys keep the last occurrence**, which is a `Map`
//! assignment and not a policy this module implements; a document with
//! duplicate keys is legal and its readings differ across libraries, so
//! the rule is stated here and held as a test rather than left to be
//! inferred from the container.

use std.bytes
use std.fmt
use std.fmt.decimal

/// A JSON value. Seven variants, one recursive type — the whole data
/// model (RFC 8259 §3-§7) with the number split the module header
/// explains.
pub enum Value {
    Null,
    Bool(bool),
    Int(int),
    Num(f64),
    Str(str),
    Arr(List[Value]),
    Obj(Map[str, Value]),
}

/// The nesting depth this module reads and renders before raising `deep` —
/// one number for `parse`, `stringify` and `stringify_pretty`, so a value
/// that came in can always go back out.
///
/// A limit rather than a stack overflow, in BOTH directions and for the
/// same reason: a JSON value can be built to any depth by a program and a
/// document can be nested to any depth by whoever sent it, and there is no
/// unwinding in wolf (D30), so a library that recurses until the stack ends
/// hands its caller a denial-of-service surface rather than a function.
/// `parse` was the sprint contract's example of the rule; `stringify`
/// shipped it first because it shipped first.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.depth_limit() == 64
/// ```
pub fn depth_limit() -> int {
    64
}

/// The JSON null.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.is_null(json.null()) == true
/// ```
pub fn null() -> Value {
    Value.Null
}

/// A JSON boolean.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.as_bool(json.from_bool(true)) else false) == true
/// json.type_name(json.from_bool(false)) == "bool"
/// ```
pub fn from_bool(b: bool) -> Value {
    Value.Bool(b)
}

/// A JSON number that is an integer, kept exact — the posture the module
/// header describes. `from_int(9007199254740993)` survives a round trip
/// through this model; the same value through `from_num` does not.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.as_int(json.from_int(42)) else 0) == 42
/// json.type_name(json.from_int(42)) == "int"
/// ```
pub fn from_int(n: int) -> Value {
    Value.Int(n)
}

/// A JSON number that is not (or need not be) an integer. Non-finite
/// inputs are legal here and render as `null`, because JSON has no
/// spelling for them — see `stringify`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.as_float(json.from_num(1.5)) else 0.0) == 1.5
/// json.type_name(json.from_num(1.5)) == "num"
/// ```
pub fn from_num(x: f64) -> Value {
    Value.Num(x)
}

/// A JSON string. The text is stored as given; escaping happens at
/// `stringify` time and never here, so a value that came from a program
/// is never double-escaped.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.as_str(json.from_str("wolf")) else "?") == "wolf"
/// json.type_name(json.from_str("")) == "str"
/// ```
pub fn from_str(s: str) -> Value {
    Value.Str(s)
}

/// A JSON array over an already-built list of values.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[json.Value]()
/// (mut xs).push(json.from_int(1))
/// let arr = json.from_list(xs)
/// (json.len(arr) else 0) == 1
/// ```
pub fn from_list(xs: List[Value]) -> Value {
    Value.Arr(xs)
}

/// A JSON object over an already-built map. The map's iteration order is
/// unspecified and so is the rendered key order (§8).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, json.Value]()
/// m["a"] = json.from_int(1)
/// let obj = json.from_map(m)
/// json.type_name(obj) == "obj"
/// ```
pub fn from_map(m: Map[str, Value]) -> Value {
    Value.Obj(m)
}

/// The variant's name — `"null"`, `"bool"`, `"int"`, `"num"`, `"str"`,
/// `"arr"` or `"obj"`. The one accessor that never fails, and the one a
/// diagnostic wants: the `kind` row tag says a value was the wrong shape,
/// and this says which shape it was. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// Spelled `type_name` and not `kind`, which is what it would be called
/// in any other library: a function and a row tag with the same name in
/// one module make the tag resolve to the FUNCTION at the raise site —
/// silently, so `as_int(json.null())` yielded a function value instead of
/// raising, and `else` never fired (F-0036). `"int"` and `"num"` are not
/// RFC 8259 type names; they are this model's, and the header explains
/// why the number split exists.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.type_name(json.null()) == "null"
/// json.type_name(json.from_str("x")) == "str"
/// ```
pub fn type_name(v: Value) -> str {
    match v {
        Null => "null",
        Bool(b) => "bool",
        Int(n) => "int",
        Num(x) => "num",
        Str(s) => "str",
        Arr(xs) => "arr",
        Obj(m) => "obj",
    }
}

/// Whether `v` is the JSON null. A predicate rather than a row, because
/// "is it null" is a question with a boolean answer (§1). Total.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.is_null(json.null()) == true
/// json.is_null(json.from_int(0)) == false
/// ```
pub fn is_null(v: Value) -> bool {
    match v {
        Null => true,
        _ => false,
    }
}

/// The boolean `v` holds; `kind` when it holds something else. No
/// coercion: `as_bool` of `Int(0)` is a miss, not `false`, because JSON's
/// types are the document's and guessing across them is how a parser
/// loses data.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.as_bool(json.from_bool(true)) else false) == true
/// (json.as_bool(json.from_int(1)) else false) == false
/// ```
pub fn as_bool(v: Value) -> bool ! {kind} {
    match v {
        Bool(b) => b,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// The integer `v` holds; `kind` for every other variant, `Num`
/// included. A `Num` that happens to be integral is still not an `Int`:
/// the model records which one the document had, and `as_float` is the
/// widening direction that always works.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.as_int(json.from_int(42)) else 0) == 42
/// (json.as_int(json.from_num(42.0)) else 0) == 0
/// ```
pub fn as_int(v: Value) -> int ! {kind} {
    match v {
        Int(n) => n,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// The number `v` holds as `f64`; `kind` for every non-numeric variant.
/// This one DOES widen: an `Int` converts, because every JSON number is a
/// number and a caller asking for `f64` has said it accepts the rounding.
/// Beyond 2^53 that rounding is lossy, which is exactly why `as_int`
/// exists and does not do it.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.as_float(json.from_num(1.5)) else 0.0) == 1.5
/// (json.as_float(json.from_int(3)) else 0.0) == 3.0
/// (json.as_float(json.from_str("3")) else 0.0) == 0.0
/// ```
pub fn as_float(v: Value) -> f64 ! {kind} {
    match v {
        Num(x) => x,
        Int(n) => n as f64,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// The string `v` holds, unescaped (the model stores text, not JSON
/// source); `kind` otherwise.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.as_str(json.from_str("wolf")) else "?") == "wolf"
/// (json.as_str(json.null()) else "?") == "?"
/// ```
pub fn as_str(v: Value) -> str ! {kind} {
    match v {
        Str(s) => s,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// The array `v` holds; `kind` otherwise. The list is the value's own —
/// reading it is free, and this module never hands out a copy it did not
/// have to make.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[json.Value]()
/// (mut xs).push(json.null())
/// let got = json.as_arr(json.from_list(xs)) else List[json.Value]()
/// got.len == 1
/// ```
pub fn as_arr(v: Value) -> List[Value] ! {kind} {
    match v {
        Arr(xs) => xs,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// The object `v` holds; `kind` otherwise.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, json.Value]()
/// m["k"] = json.from_int(1)
/// let got = json.as_obj(json.from_map(m)) else Map[str, json.Value]()
/// got.len == 1
/// ```
pub fn as_obj(v: Value) -> Map[str, Value] ! {kind} {
    match v {
        Obj(m) => m,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// Whether `v` is an object with an entry at `key`. `false` for every
/// other variant, because "does this document have this field" has a
/// useful answer for a non-object too, and it is no.
///
/// It survives `get`'s arrival rather than being replaced by it, and the
/// reason is §1's: a question with a boolean answer keeps its predicate.
/// `has` is what a caller writes when the value is not wanted (a schema
/// check, a "did this field appear at all" branch over a `null` that IS
/// present); `get` is what a caller writes when it is.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, json.Value]()
/// m["a"] = json.from_int(7)
/// let obj = json.from_map(m)
/// json.has(obj, "a") == true
/// json.has(obj, "b") == false
/// json.has(json.from_int(1), "a") == false
/// ```
pub fn has(v: Value, key: str) -> bool {
    match v {
        Obj(m) => obj_has(m, key),
        _ => false,
    }
}

/// The lookup itself. Private, and a linear scan for two recorded
/// reasons: the builtin `Map` has no `has` and its absent-key read
/// answers `()` rather than raising (F-0011), and `std.map.has` — the
/// std function whose job this is — carries an `Eq` bound, so calling it
/// is "`Eq` is a trait; traits, enums and type-level items have no
/// dynamic semantics here" (F-0002). Comparing `str` keys with `==` runs;
/// this body flips back to `map.has` the day trait dispatch does.
fn obj_has(m: Map[str, Value], key: str) -> bool {
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        if pair.0 == key {
            return true
        }
    }
    false
}

/// The value at `key`; `none` when the object has no entry there, `kind`
/// when `v` is not an object at all.
///
/// **Two tags rather than one, because the two questions are different.**
/// "This document has no `port` field" is a thing a caller routinely plans
/// for; "the thing you are indexing is a number" is a caller's mistake
/// about its own data — and §12 keeps them apart so an `else` can too. A
/// caller that genuinely does not care writes `else json.null()`.
///
/// Bare `get` with a row is §1's checked-access spelling. There is no
/// `get_` prefix and no unchecked twin: an absent key is not a fault
/// (§8), so the trapping direction this function could have had does not
/// exist and never will.
///
/// **This function and `at` were written at sc05, tested, and withdrawn
/// for five sprints** — a `Value` returned through an error row took the
/// miss path on every call, silently (F-0037, closed at lupin 0.1.10 and
/// re-measured at 0.1.11 and again at 0.1.12). Nothing about the signature
/// changed in the meantime, which is the point: the contract in the module
/// header was right, and what was missing was a machine that ran it.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, json.Value]()
/// m["a"] = json.from_int(7)
/// m["n"] = json.null()
/// let obj = json.from_map(m)
/// let a = json.get(obj, "a") else json.from_int(0 - 1)
/// (json.as_int(a) else 0) == 7
/// let missing = json.get(obj, "zz") else json.from_str("absent")
/// (json.as_str(missing) else "?") == "absent"
/// let n = json.get(obj, "n") else json.from_int(0 - 1)
/// json.is_null(n) == true
/// let wrong = json.get(json.from_int(1), "a") else json.from_str("not an object")
/// (json.as_str(wrong) else "?") == "not an object"
/// ```
pub fn get(v: Value, key: str) -> Value ! {kind, none} {
    match v {
        Obj(m) => obj_get(m, key)?,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// The lookup itself. Private, and a linear scan for `obj_has`'s two
/// recorded reasons (F-0011's absent-key read, F-0002's `Eq` bound).
///
/// A duplicate key cannot occur in a `Map`, so "the last one wins" is a
/// rule `parse` will enforce on the way IN rather than one this reader has
/// to state.
fn obj_get(m: Map[str, Value], key: str) -> Value ! {none} {
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        if pair.0 == key {
            return pair.1
        }
    }
    none
}

/// The element at `i`; `none` when `i` is outside the array, `kind` when
/// `v` is not an array.
///
/// **A row where `List` traps** (§8, and this is the worked example of
/// its rule): an index into a document is DATA — the caller could not have
/// checked what the document contains, because reading the document is
/// what it is doing — so an out-of-range index is an outcome this function
/// reports and never a process this function ends. A negative index is the
/// same miss and not a from-the-end read; `^n` does not exist in wolf and
/// inventing it here would be a second dialect.
///
/// Withdrawn with `get` at sc05 and shipped with it now; see `get`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[json.Value]()
/// (mut xs).push(json.from_str("first"))
/// (mut xs).push(json.null())
/// let arr = json.from_list(xs)
/// let a = json.at(arr, 0) else json.from_str("?")
/// (json.as_str(a) else "?") == "first"
/// let past = json.at(arr, 2) else json.from_str("past the end")
/// (json.as_str(past) else "?") == "past the end"
/// let below = json.at(arr, 0 - 1) else json.from_str("below zero")
/// (json.as_str(below) else "?") == "below zero"
/// let wrong = json.at(json.from_int(1), 0) else json.from_str("not an array")
/// (json.as_str(wrong) else "?") == "not an array"
/// ```
pub fn at(v: Value, i: int) -> Value ! {kind, none} {
    match v {
        Arr(xs) => arr_at(xs, i)?,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// The indexing itself, with the bounds test the row is made of. Private.
fn arr_at(xs: List[Value], i: int) -> Value ! {none} {
    if i < 0 {
        return none
    }
    if i >= xs.len {
        return none
    }
    xs[i]
}

/// The keys of an object, in the map's unspecified order; `kind` for
/// every other variant. Allocates the list (`to_`-grade, §1).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, json.Value]()
/// m["only"] = json.null()
/// let ks = json.keys(json.from_map(m)) else List[str]()
/// ks.len == 1
/// let none_here = json.keys(json.from_int(1)) else List[str]()
/// none_here.len == 0
/// ```
pub fn keys(v: Value) -> List[str] ! {kind} {
    match v {
        Obj(m) => obj_keys(m),
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// The key list. Private.
fn obj_keys(m: Map[str, Value]) -> List[str] {
    var out = List[str]()
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        (mut out).push(pair.0)
    }
    out
}

/// The number of elements in an array or entries in an object; `kind` for
/// every scalar. Not a string length: JSON strings are text and
/// `std.str.len` is where text length lives.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[json.Value]()
/// (mut xs).push(json.null())
/// (mut xs).push(json.null())
/// (json.len(json.from_list(xs)) else 0) == 2
/// (json.len(json.from_int(1)) else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// ```
pub fn len(v: Value) -> int ! {kind} {
    match v {
        Arr(xs) => xs.len,
        Obj(m) => m.len,
        _ => kind,
    }
}

/// `s` parsed as one JSON document — the direction this module could not
/// go for eight sprints.
///
/// **The profile is RFC 8259, strictly**, and the module header lists what
/// that excludes. Whitespace (space, tab, CR, LF) is allowed around every
/// token and nowhere inside one; trailing content after the value is a
/// miss, so `"1 2"` does not parse as `1`.
///
/// **Numbers keep the posture the model was built for**: text that denotes
/// an integer `int` can hold becomes `Int` and keeps every digit, and
/// everything else becomes `Num` through `std.fmt.decimal.parse_float`,
/// which is correctly rounded. So a document carrying a 64-bit id survives
/// a round trip and `1e2` arrives as the double `100.0` rather than as the
/// integer 100 — the text said it was not an integer.
///
/// **Three tags.** `syntax` is a malformed document. `deep` is nesting
/// past `depth_limit()`, raised rather than recursing until the stack
/// ends: an untrusted document must not be able to end the process, which
/// is the same argument `stringify` makes from the writing side. `overflow`
/// is a number whose magnitude has no `f64` (`1e400`) — the number tier
/// refuses it rather than answering infinity, and this module refuses to
/// call that a malformed document.
///
/// A duplicate key keeps the LAST occurrence.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let v = json.parse("[1, true, null]") else json.null()
/// (json.len(v) else 0) == 3
/// let n = json.parse("  42  ") else json.null()
/// (json.as_int(n) else 0) == 42
/// let s = json.parse("\"a\\nb\"") else json.null()
/// (json.as_str(s) else "?") == "a\nb"
/// let bad = json.parse("[1,]") else json.from_str("not json")
/// (json.as_str(bad) else "?") == "not json"
/// ```
pub fn parse(s: str) -> Value ! {syntax, deep, overflow} {
    var c = Cursor { text: s, i: 0 }
    skip_ws(mut c)
    let v = parse_value(mut c, 0)?
    skip_ws(mut c)
    if c.i < c.text.len {
        return syntax
    }
    v
}

/// The scanner's position in the text it is reading. Private, and it is
/// deliberately a `str` and an offset rather than a byte list: a
/// `List[int]` INDEX READ is O(n) under the interpreter (F-0078), which is
/// this module's only executing lane, so a scanner built on `bs[i]` would
/// be quadratic in the document. `text.get(i..i + 1)` is O(1) there,
/// measured, and it is what `byte_at` below is written over.
struct Cursor {
    text: str,
    i: int,
}

/// The byte at `at`, or -1 when `at` is past the end OR inside a code
/// point. Private, and the conflation is deliberate: every byte this
/// scanner tests for is ASCII, no byte of a multi-byte code point is
/// ASCII, and a position inside one is therefore "not a delimiter" — the
/// same answer as "not there". It is what makes the whole scanner total
/// over arbitrary UTF-8 without measuring a single code point.
///
/// The first byte comes from ITERATING a one-character slice, not from
/// indexing it. `s.bytes()[0]` was refused by wolfc's checked tier when
/// this was written (F-0071, closed at the sc12 pin) and a shape that costs
/// a lane is not one std writes even in a module whose other lanes are
/// already dark; the loop is kept because nothing about it got worse.
fn byte_at(text: str, at: int) -> int {
    let ch = text.get(at..at + 1) else ""
    for b in ch.bytes() {
        return b as int
    }
    0 - 1
}

/// Past any run of the four characters RFC 8259 calls whitespace. Private.
fn skip_ws(mut c: Cursor) {
    var going = true
    while going {
        let b = byte_at(c.text, c.i)
        if b == 32 || b == 9 || b == 10 || b == 13 {
            c.i = c.i + 1
        } else {
            going = false
        }
    }
}

/// One value, dispatched on its first byte. Private; `depth` is checked on
/// entry so a deeply nested document raises `deep` instead of exhausting
/// the stack.
fn parse_value(mut c: Cursor, depth: int) -> Value ! {syntax, deep, overflow} {
    if depth > depth_limit() {
        return deep
    }
    let b = byte_at(c.text, c.i)
    if b == 123 {
        let obj = parse_object(mut c, depth)?
        return obj
    }
    if b == 91 {
        let arr = parse_array(mut c, depth)?
        return arr
    }
    if b == 34 {
        let text = parse_string(mut c)?
        return Value.Str(text)
    }
    if b == 116 {
        if !take_word(mut c, "true") {
            return syntax
        }
        return Value.Bool(true)
    }
    if b == 102 {
        if !take_word(mut c, "false") {
            return syntax
        }
        return Value.Bool(false)
    }
    if b == 110 {
        if !take_word(mut c, "null") {
            return syntax
        }
        return Value.Null
    }
    if b == 45 || is_digit_byte(b) {
        let num = parse_number(mut c)?
        return num
    }
    syntax
}

/// An array body, cursor on the `[`. Private. A trailing comma is a miss
/// because the element after it is not a value.
fn parse_array(mut c: Cursor, depth: int) -> Value ! {syntax, deep, overflow} {
    c.i = c.i + 1
    var out = List[Value]()
    skip_ws(mut c)
    if byte_at(c.text, c.i) == 93 {
        c.i = c.i + 1
        return Value.Arr(out)
    }
    var going = true
    while going {
        skip_ws(mut c)
        let v = parse_value(mut c, depth + 1)?
        (mut out).push(v)
        skip_ws(mut c)
        let b = byte_at(c.text, c.i)
        if b == 44 {
            c.i = c.i + 1
        } else if b == 93 {
            c.i = c.i + 1
            going = false
        } else {
            return syntax
        }
    }
    Value.Arr(out)
}

/// An object body, cursor on the `{`. Private. A key must be a string —
/// a bare identifier is JavaScript, not JSON — and the last occurrence of
/// a duplicate key is what the `Map` keeps.
fn parse_object(mut c: Cursor, depth: int) -> Value ! {syntax, deep, overflow} {
    c.i = c.i + 1
    var m = Map[str, Value]()
    skip_ws(mut c)
    if byte_at(c.text, c.i) == 125 {
        c.i = c.i + 1
        return Value.Obj(m)
    }
    var going = true
    while going {
        skip_ws(mut c)
        if byte_at(c.text, c.i) != 34 {
            return syntax
        }
        let key = parse_string(mut c)?
        skip_ws(mut c)
        if byte_at(c.text, c.i) != 58 {
            return syntax
        }
        c.i = c.i + 1
        skip_ws(mut c)
        let v = parse_value(mut c, depth + 1)?
        m[key] = v
        skip_ws(mut c)
        let b = byte_at(c.text, c.i)
        if b == 44 {
            c.i = c.i + 1
        } else if b == 125 {
            c.i = c.i + 1
            going = false
        } else {
            return syntax
        }
    }
    Value.Obj(m)
}

/// A string payload, cursor on the opening quote, returned UNESCAPED.
/// Private.
///
/// The scan finds the closing quote and nothing else: a backslash consumes
/// the byte after it (so an escaped quote does not end the string) and
/// every other byte is content. A raw control character below U+0020 is a
/// miss, which is RFC 8259's rule and the one place this module is
/// stricter than the parsers that let a raw newline through. The escapes
/// themselves are `unescape`'s business, on the span this found.
fn parse_string(mut c: Cursor) -> str ! {syntax} {
    let n = c.text.len
    let start = c.i + 1
    var j = start
    var end = start
    var closed = false
    while j < n {
        let b = byte_at(c.text, j)
        if b == 92 {
            if j + 1 >= n {
                return syntax
            }
            j = j + 2
        } else if b == 34 {
            end = j
            closed = true
            j = n
        } else if b >= 0 && b < 32 {
            return syntax
        } else {
            j = j + 1
        }
    }
    if !closed {
        return syntax
    }
    let raw = c.text[start..end]
    c.i = end + 1
    unescape(raw)?
}

/// A number, cursor on its first byte. Private.
///
/// The grammar is checked before the text is converted — `-`? then `0` or
/// a nonzero digit run, then an optional fraction with at least one digit,
/// then an optional exponent with at least one digit — so `01`, `.5`, `1.`
/// , `+1` and `1e` are all misses rather than partial reads. Which
/// CONSTRUCTOR the text gets is decided by the text: an integer that fits
/// `int` keeps every digit in `Int`, and everything else goes through
/// `parse_float`.
///
/// **The handler on `parse_float` is a wildcard, deliberately.** That call
/// declares `{parse, overflow}` and only `overflow` is reachable from here:
/// the grammar above accepts a strict subset of what `parse_float` reads,
/// so text that got this far cannot be text it rejects. A discriminating
/// handler is not needed for a different reason than it once was: F-0079
/// (the first-arm bug this comment used to cite) is FIXED at lupin
/// 0.1.13, and `tests/json/parse_misses.lu` now holds the three-arm
/// discrimination this module's row supports. The wildcard stays because
/// §14's rule still applies on its own terms: a module keeps a wildcard
/// when it cannot act on the difference, and here it cannot see it
/// either.
fn parse_number(mut c: Cursor) -> Value ! {syntax, overflow} {
    let start = c.i
    var j = c.i
    if byte_at(c.text, j) == 45 {
        j = j + 1
    }
    let first = byte_at(c.text, j)
    if first == 48 {
        j = j + 1
    } else if first >= 49 && first <= 57 {
        j = j + 1
        while is_digit_byte(byte_at(c.text, j)) {
            j = j + 1
        }
    } else {
        return syntax
    }
    var integral = true
    if byte_at(c.text, j) == 46 {
        integral = false
        j = j + 1
        if !is_digit_byte(byte_at(c.text, j)) {
            return syntax
        }
        while is_digit_byte(byte_at(c.text, j)) {
            j = j + 1
        }
    }
    let marker = byte_at(c.text, j)
    if marker == 101 || marker == 69 {
        integral = false
        j = j + 1
        let sign = byte_at(c.text, j)
        if sign == 43 || sign == 45 {
            j = j + 1
        }
        if !is_digit_byte(byte_at(c.text, j)) {
            return syntax
        }
        while is_digit_byte(byte_at(c.text, j)) {
            j = j + 1
        }
    }
    let text = c.text[start..j]
    c.i = j
    if integral {
        if fits_int(text) {
            let whole = fmt.parse_int(text) else 0
            return Value.Int(whole)
        }
    }
    let x = decimal.parse_float(text) else |_| {
        return overflow
    }
    Value.Num(x)
}

/// `word` at the cursor, consumed. Private, and the whole of `true`,
/// `false` and `null`: a prefix match would accept `truex`, so the caller
/// checks what follows by parsing it as the next token.
fn take_word(mut c: Cursor, word: str) -> bool {
    let end = c.i + word.len
    if end > c.text.len {
        return false
    }
    let got = c.text.get(c.i..end) else ""
    if got == word {
        c.i = end
        return true
    }
    false
}

/// Whether `b` is an ASCII digit. Private.
fn is_digit_byte(b: int) -> bool {
    b >= 48 && b <= 57
}

/// Whether the integer text `text` denotes fits in `int`. Private, and the
/// gate in front of `fmt.parse_int`, which TRAPS `overflow` on text that
/// does not fit — correctly, because to `fmt` the digits are a caller's
/// argument. Here they came out of a DOCUMENT, so §10's rule applies from
/// the other side: data raises where a caller's mistake traps, and what
/// this module does with a number too big for `int` is not to raise at all
/// but to keep it as a `Num`.
///
/// The comparison is on the text: 19 digits is the boundary width, and at
/// exactly 19 the leading 18 are compared against `int`'s own, with the
/// negative bound one larger. Leading zeros cannot appear — the grammar
/// above rejects them — so a width comparison is exact.
fn fits_int(text: str) -> bool {
    var body = text
    var negative = false
    if body.starts_with("-") {
        negative = true
        body = body[1..]
    }
    let width = body.len
    if width == 0 {
        return false
    }
    if width < 19 {
        return true
    }
    if width > 19 {
        return false
    }
    let head_text = body[0..18]
    let tail_text = body[18..19]
    let head = fmt.parse_int(head_text) else 0 - 1
    let tail = fmt.parse_int(tail_text) else 0 - 1
    if head < 0 {
        return false
    }
    if tail < 0 {
        return false
    }
    let head_limit: int = 922337203685477580
    var tail_limit: int = 7
    if negative {
        tail_limit = 8
    }
    if head > head_limit {
        return false
    }
    if head == head_limit {
        return tail <= tail_limit
    }
    true
}

/// `s` with the characters JSON forbids inside a string replaced by their
/// escapes: `"` and `\` by their backslash forms, and every control
/// character below U+0020 by `\b`, `\f`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t` or `\u00XX`.
/// Every other character — including every non-ASCII one — passes through
/// verbatim, because RFC 8259 permits raw UTF-8 and escaping it would only
/// make the output bigger.
///
/// **Total, as of sc14, and its `boundary` tag is gone.** For five sprints
/// this function refused a string that held a non-ASCII code point AND,
/// after it, a character needing an escape: the walk stepped one ASCII
/// character at a time and nothing could measure a wider one. What removed
/// the refusal is not the code-point primitive the old contract named but
/// a change of QUESTION. The four characters that must be escaped are
/// ASCII; every byte of a multi-byte code point is ≥ 0x80 and so is never
/// one of them; a run between two escapes is therefore a slice at two
/// offsets THIS function computed, and a byte walk needs no code-point
/// measurement at all. The old body was answering a harder question than
/// the job asked.
///
/// The removal is a signature change and the module header says so:
/// `stringify` and `stringify_pretty` lose `boundary` from their rows.
///
/// A string needing no escape at all comes back unchanged, and the fast
/// path is the walk finding nothing rather than a second pass.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// json.escape("wolf") == "wolf"
/// json.escape("café") == "café"
/// json.escape("café \"x\"") == "café \\\"x\\\""
/// json.escape("a\"b") == "a\\\"b"
/// json.escape("a\\b") == "a\\\\b"
/// json.escape("a\nb") == "a\\nb"
/// ```
pub fn escape(s: str) -> str {
    var out = ""
    var start: int = 0
    var i: int = 0
    for raw in s.bytes() {
        let mapped = byte_escape(raw as int)
        if !mapped.is_empty() {
            let run = s[start..i]
            out = "{out}{run}{mapped}"
            start = i + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if start == 0 {
        return s
    }
    let tail = s[start..s.len]
    out + tail
}

/// The JSON escape for one byte, or the empty string when the byte passes
/// through. Private. Every byte of a multi-byte code point lands in the
/// last case, which is why the walk above never has to know it was inside
/// one.
fn byte_escape(b: int) -> str {
    if b == 34 {
        return "\\\""
    }
    if b == 92 {
        return "\\\\"
    }
    if b >= 32 {
        return ""
    }
    control_escape(b)
}

/// `s` with JSON's escapes resolved — `escape`'s inverse, and the function
/// `parse` reads every string payload through.
///
/// The eight two-character escapes (`\"`, `\\`, `\/`, `\b`, `\f`, `\n`,
/// `\r`, `\t`) and `\uXXXX`, with a surrogate PAIR joined into the one code
/// point it denotes. Any other character after a backslash is a `syntax`
/// miss, and so is a lone surrogate, a truncated `\u`, and a trailing
/// backslash. Text with no backslash in it comes back unchanged.
///
/// **The tag is `syntax` and not the `parse` its sc05 contract named**,
/// because this module now has a FUNCTION called `parse` and a row tag
/// that shares a name with anything in scope resolves to that thing —
/// silently, with no `else` firing and no diagnostic (F-0036, re-measured
/// under lupin at this sprint's pins and still live there). The rename is
/// also the better name.
///
/// **What it waited four sprints for.** Decoding `é` means building
/// `"é"` out of the number 233, and nothing in the language built a `str`
/// from a number until s81's validating `str_from_utf8` (F-0057, closed at
/// the sc13 pin, wrapped as `std.bytes.to_str`). An ASCII-only version
/// would have refused text, which §9 forbids.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.unescape("wolf") else "?") == "wolf"
/// (json.unescape("a\\nb") else "?") == "a\nb"
/// (json.unescape("a\\\"b") else "?") == "a\"b"
/// (json.unescape("\\u00e9") else "?") == "é"
/// (json.unescape("\\ud83d\\udc3a") else "?") == "🐺"
/// (json.unescape("a\\qb") else "bad escape") == "bad escape"
/// (json.unescape("\\ud800") else "lone surrogate") == "lone surrogate"
/// ```
pub fn unescape(s: str) -> str ! {syntax} {
    let n = s.len
    var out = ""
    var start: int = 0
    var i: int = 0
    while i < n {
        if byte_at(s, i) == 92 {
            let (text, past) = escape_at(s, i)?
            let run = s[start..i]
            out = "{out}{run}{text}"
            i = past
            start = i
        } else {
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    if start == 0 {
        return s
    }
    let tail = s[start..n]
    out + tail
}

/// The text one escape at `at` denotes, and the offset just past it.
/// Private. A tuple rather than two functions because the two answers are
/// one reading, and a `(str, int)` through a row runs on every lane that
/// has rows at all (sc09 measured the `(str, str)` half).
fn escape_at(text: str, at: int) -> (str, int) ! {syntax} {
    let n = text.len
    if at + 1 >= n {
        return syntax
    }
    let e = byte_at(text, at + 1)
    if e == 34 {
        return ("\"", at + 2)
    }
    if e == 92 {
        return ("\\", at + 2)
    }
    if e == 47 {
        return ("/", at + 2)
    }
    if e == 98 {
        return ("\x08", at + 2)
    }
    if e == 102 {
        return ("\x0c", at + 2)
    }
    if e == 110 {
        return ("\n", at + 2)
    }
    if e == 114 {
        return ("\r", at + 2)
    }
    if e == 116 {
        return ("\t", at + 2)
    }
    if e != 117 {
        return syntax
    }
    let first = hex4(text, at + 2)?
    if first < 55296 || first > 57343 {
        let one = utf8_of(first)?
        return (one, at + 6)
    }
    if first > 56319 {
        return syntax
    }
    if at + 7 >= n {
        return syntax
    }
    if byte_at(text, at + 6) != 92 {
        return syntax
    }
    if byte_at(text, at + 7) != 117 {
        return syntax
    }
    let second = hex4(text, at + 8)?
    if second < 56320 || second > 57343 {
        return syntax
    }
    let joined = 65536 + (first - 55296) * 1024 + (second - 56320)
    let pair = utf8_of(joined)?
    (pair, at + 12)
}

/// The four hex digits at `at` as a number. Private; upper and lower case
/// both, as RFC 8259 requires, and fewer than four digits is a miss.
fn hex4(text: str, at: int) -> int ! {syntax} {
    if at + 4 > text.len {
        return syntax
    }
    var v: int = 0
    var k: int = 0
    while k < 4 {
        let d = hex_value(byte_at(text, at + k))
        if d < 0 {
            return syntax
        }
        v = v * 16 + d
        k = k + 1
    }
    v
}

/// The value of one hex digit byte, or -1. Private.
fn hex_value(b: int) -> int {
    if b >= 48 && b <= 57 {
        return b - 48
    }
    if b >= 97 && b <= 102 {
        return b - 87
    }
    if b >= 65 && b <= 70 {
        return b - 55
    }
    0 - 1
}

/// The code point `cp` as text. Private, and the function F-0057 blocked:
/// the UTF-8 arithmetic was always writable here and its OUTPUT had
/// nowhere to go until `str_from_utf8` landed.
///
/// The `syntax` fallback is the primitive's own validation, and it is
/// unreachable through this door: `escape_at` filters the surrogate range
/// before calling, and every other scalar `\uXXXX` can name encodes to
/// well-formed UTF-8. It is kept rather than swallowed because a wrapper
/// that discards a validating primitive's row is asserting where it could
/// be checking — the same rule `std.bytes.to_str`'s test is built on.
fn utf8_of(cp: int) -> str ! {syntax} {
    var b = List[byte]()
    if cp < 128 {
        (mut b).push(cp as byte)
    } else if cp < 2048 {
        (mut b).push((192 + cp / 64) as byte)
        (mut b).push((128 + cp % 64) as byte)
    } else if cp < 65536 {
        (mut b).push((224 + cp / 4096) as byte)
        (mut b).push((128 + cp / 64 % 64) as byte)
        (mut b).push((128 + cp % 64) as byte)
    } else {
        (mut b).push((240 + cp / 262144) as byte)
        (mut b).push((128 + cp / 4096 % 64) as byte)
        (mut b).push((128 + cp / 64 % 64) as byte)
        (mut b).push((128 + cp % 64) as byte)
    }
    bytes.to_str(b) else |_| {
        return syntax
    }
}

/// The escape for the control character with code `i`: the five
/// two-character forms JSON names, and `\u00XX` for the rest. Private.
fn control_escape(i: int) -> str {
    if i == 8 {
        return "\\b"
    }
    if i == 9 {
        return "\\t"
    }
    if i == 10 {
        return "\\n"
    }
    if i == 12 {
        return "\\f"
    }
    if i == 13 {
        return "\\r"
    }
    let digits = fmt.pad_left_with(fmt.to_hex(i), 4, "0")
    "\\u{digits}"
}

/// `v` rendered as compact JSON text — no spaces, no newlines, keys in
/// the map's unspecified order. One tag: `deep`, when nesting passes
/// `depth_limit()`. It used to be two — `boundary` left with sc14's total
/// `escape`, and a caller that named that tag is the one call site this
/// module's history breaks.
///
/// Numbers: an `Int` renders as a decimal integer, exactly; a `Num`
/// renders through `std.fmt.decimal.to_str`, the shortest text that reads
/// back as the same double — so `stringify` of a document a program built
/// loses nothing. A non-finite `Num` renders as `null`, because JSON has
/// no spelling for infinity or NaN and the alternative (a raise, or
/// inventing `1e999`) is worse for the caller and worse for the reader;
/// JavaScript's own `JSON.stringify` made the same choice.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.stringify(json.null()) else "?") == "null"
/// (json.stringify(json.from_bool(true)) else "?") == "true"
/// (json.stringify(json.from_int(42)) else "?") == "42"
/// (json.stringify(json.from_num(1.5)) else "?") == "1.5"
/// (json.stringify(json.from_str("hi")) else "?") == "\"hi\""
/// var xs = List[json.Value]()
/// (mut xs).push(json.from_int(1))
/// (mut xs).push(json.null())
/// (json.stringify(json.from_list(xs)) else "?") == "[1,null]"
/// ```
pub fn stringify(v: Value) -> str ! {deep} {
    render(v, 0)?
}

/// `v` rendered as JSON text with `indent` spaces per nesting level and
/// one entry per line — the human-readable form. An `indent` of zero
/// gives newlines without indentation; a negative `indent` traps
/// `assert`. Empty arrays and objects stay on one line (`[]`, `{}`),
/// which is what every pretty-printer does and what every diff wants.
/// The rows are `stringify`'s.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (json.stringify_pretty(json.from_int(1), 2) else "?") == "1"
/// var xs = List[json.Value]()
/// (mut xs).push(json.from_int(1))
/// (json.stringify_pretty(json.from_list(xs), 2) else "?") == "[\n  1\n]"
/// var empty = List[json.Value]()
/// (json.stringify_pretty(json.from_list(empty), 2) else "?") == "[]"
/// ```
pub fn stringify_pretty(v: Value, indent: int) -> str ! {deep} {
    if indent < 0 {
        assert(false)
    }
    render_pretty(v, indent, 0)?
}

/// The compact renderer. Private; `depth` is checked against
/// `depth_limit()` on entry so that a cycle-free but very deep value
/// raises instead of exhausting the stack.
fn render(v: Value, depth: int) -> str ! {deep} {
    if depth > depth_limit() {
        return deep
    }
    match v {
        Null => "null",
        Bool(b) => "{b}",
        Int(n) => fmt.to_str(n),
        Num(x) => render_num(x),
        Str(s) => quote(s),
        Arr(xs) => render_arr(xs, depth)?,
        Obj(m) => render_obj(m, depth)?,
    }
}

/// A `Num`'s text: the shortest round-tripping decimal, or `null` for the
/// values JSON cannot spell. Private.
fn render_num(x: f64) -> str {
    let text = decimal.to_str(x)
    if text == "nan" {
        return "null"
    }
    if text == "inf" {
        return "null"
    }
    if text == "-inf" {
        return "null"
    }
    text
}

/// A string payload, escaped and quoted. Private.
fn quote(s: str) -> str {
    let body = escape(s)
    "\"{body}\""
}

/// The compact array body. Private.
fn render_arr(xs: List[Value], depth: int) -> str ! {deep} {
    var out = "["
    let n = xs.len
    var i: int = 0
    while i < n {
        let piece = render(xs[i], depth + 1)?
        if i == 0 {
            out += piece
        } else {
            out = "{out},{piece}"
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    "{out}]"
}

/// The compact object body. Private.
fn render_obj(m: Map[str, Value], depth: int) -> str ! {deep} {
    var out = "{{"
    var first = true
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        let key = quote(pair.0)
        let piece = render(pair.1, depth + 1)?
        if first {
            out = "{out}{key}:{piece}"
            first = false
        } else {
            out = "{out},{key}:{piece}"
        }
    }
    "{out}}}"
}

/// The indented renderer. Private.
fn render_pretty(v: Value, indent: int, depth: int) -> str ! {deep} {
    if depth > depth_limit() {
        return deep
    }
    match v {
        Arr(xs) => pretty_arr(xs, indent, depth)?,
        Obj(m) => pretty_obj(m, indent, depth)?,
        _ => render(v, depth)?,
    }
}

/// `indent * level` spaces. Private.
fn pad(indent: int, level: int) -> str {
    let width = indent * level
    if width <= 0 {
        return ""
    }
    " ".repeat(width)
}

/// The indented array body. Private.
fn pretty_arr(xs: List[Value], indent: int, depth: int) -> str ! {deep} {
    let n = xs.len
    if n == 0 {
        return "[]"
    }
    let inner = pad(indent, depth + 1)
    let outer = pad(indent, depth)
    var out = "["
    var i: int = 0
    while i < n {
        let piece = render_pretty(xs[i], indent, depth + 1)?
        if i == 0 {
            out = "{out}\n{inner}{piece}"
        } else {
            out = "{out},\n{inner}{piece}"
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    "{out}\n{outer}]"
}

/// The indented object body. Private.
fn pretty_obj(m: Map[str, Value], indent: int, depth: int) -> str ! {deep} {
    if m.len == 0 {
        return "{{}}"
    }
    let inner = pad(indent, depth + 1)
    let outer = pad(indent, depth)
    var out = "{{"
    var first = true
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        let key = quote(pair.0)
        let piece = render_pretty(pair.1, indent, depth + 1)?
        if first {
            out = "{out}\n{inner}{key}: {piece}"
            first = false
        } else {
            out = "{out},\n{inner}{key}: {piece}"
        }
    }
    "{out}\n{outer}}}"
}
